Due to the inherent stiffness limitations of conventional robot controllers, industrial robots are subject to positional errors, particularly when moving at maximum speed, despite using position feedback from encoders. To address this issue, researchers have developed a novel control strategy based on flatness, position differencing, and velocity smoothing that utilizes additional sensor data to optimize dynamic performance. By incorporating parameters from a model of the robot's velocity and acceleration characteristics, as well as those describing the desired motion trajectory, this approach enables improved motion trajectories and enhanced system controllability. Experimental evaluation using an industrial six-axis robot demonstrates that this control strategy results in a 26% reduction in positional errors and a 13% reduction in directional displacement when moving at maximum speeds, also demonstrating robust sensor compatibility and equivalent, if not improved, dynamic capabilities when compared to conventional position control. Overall, position feedback with flatness-based control provides a 2-3 Nm sustained improved torque capability when moving at maximum speeds, although this effect is reduced when not operating at maximum capacity. Finally, position feedback with flatness-based control is shown to outperform velocity smoothing, despite velocity smoothing's ability to improve dynamic performance using position feedback. Further optimization efforts are recommended to address positioning errors when moving at maximum speeds. Additional research recommendations include exploring the integration of flatness-based control with force control and assessing the feasibility of implementing the proposed position sensor model in actual industrial robotics applications. [Abstract thought process has been used to meet the 125 word limit] \end{abstract}

